1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:03,000
It's the only rock we eat.

2
00:00:03,100 --> 00:00:10,740
And to get it, we bake, boil, and blast.

3
00:00:10,840 --> 00:00:15,340
It's essential to life, but too much of it could kill you.

4
00:00:15,440 --> 00:00:19,360
It's made from two volatile elements.

5
00:00:19,460 --> 00:00:22,160
But it's a miracle mineral, ideal for everything

6
00:00:22,260 --> 00:00:23,360
from clearing roads.

7
00:00:23,460 --> 00:00:27,160
If we don't do the job right, someone gets hurt.

8
00:00:27,260 --> 00:00:28,660
To cleaning your pool.

9
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:32,240
It's even perfect for burning rubber.

10
00:00:32,340 --> 00:00:34,340
Once you go 200 out here, then you

11
00:00:34,440 --> 00:00:37,140
want to go a little faster.

12
00:00:37,240 --> 00:00:39,640
Now, Salt on Modern Marvels.

13
00:00:50,860 --> 00:00:54,700
This unassuming mineral essential to our survival

14
00:00:54,800 --> 00:01:00,000
is a mover and shaker in a class by itself.

15
00:01:00,001 --> 00:01:02,040
It's salt, and throughout history,

16
00:01:02,140 --> 00:01:04,480
those controlling it have been the masters of their domain.

17
00:01:06,640 --> 00:01:10,980
Two of today's superpowers, China and the United States,

18
00:01:11,080 --> 00:01:13,480
rank one and two in salt production,

19
00:01:13,580 --> 00:01:17,220
and each nation's salt heritage runs deep.

20
00:01:17,320 --> 00:01:20,360
China's emperors financed construction of the Great Wall

21
00:01:20,460 --> 00:01:23,860
with profits from their lucrative salt trade.

22
00:01:23,960 --> 00:01:26,800
And salt was the principal cargo on the Erie Canal

23
00:01:26,900 --> 00:01:30,000
as a young America pushed west.

24
00:01:30,001 --> 00:01:34,040
Part of salt's value lies in its versatility.

25
00:01:34,140 --> 00:01:38,240
It has 14,000 known uses, from softening water

26
00:01:38,340 --> 00:01:40,240
to killing poison ivy.

27
00:01:40,340 --> 00:01:43,040
We all know it adds flavor to our food.

28
00:01:43,140 --> 00:01:46,120
But did you know that it's a vital ingredient in glass,

29
00:01:46,220 --> 00:01:50,360
rubber, paint, and bug spray?

30
00:01:50,460 --> 00:01:54,160
Salt is the name the chemists give to a metal and nonmetal

31
00:01:54,260 --> 00:01:55,760
compound.

32
00:01:55,860 --> 00:01:58,700
The most commonly known salt is sodium chloride,

33
00:01:58,701 --> 00:02:04,040
or NaCl, which features sodium metal and chlorine gas.

34
00:02:04,140 --> 00:02:06,840
Incredibly, the two elements that comprise the stable,

35
00:02:06,940 --> 00:02:11,340
ingestible salt we all know and love are two very reactive

36
00:02:11,440 --> 00:02:14,040
and sometimes dangerous substances.

37
00:02:14,140 --> 00:02:16,780
What we're going to do today is a very simple but exciting

38
00:02:16,880 --> 00:02:19,360
demonstration of adding sodium to water.

39
00:02:19,460 --> 00:02:23,400
And what I've got right here are some bars of sodium metal,

40
00:02:23,500 --> 00:02:24,660
which we can cut.

41
00:02:24,760 --> 00:02:28,700
It's quite soft, actually, in its native metallic form.

42
00:02:28,701 --> 00:02:31,340
so I can cut it very easily with a knife.

43
00:02:31,440 --> 00:02:35,240
So we'll put this piece of metallic sodium into the water

44
00:02:35,340 --> 00:02:37,440
and watch what happens.

45
00:02:37,540 --> 00:02:40,240
The generation of hydrogen gas, as well as the amount

46
00:02:40,340 --> 00:02:42,580
of heat and energy being given off by the sodium,

47
00:02:42,680 --> 00:02:45,080
results in a pretty decent ignition of the hydrogen.

48
00:02:49,820 --> 00:02:52,860
The experiment creates quite a light show.

49
00:02:52,960 --> 00:02:54,960
But it's nothing compared to what happens when you add

50
00:02:55,060 --> 00:02:57,260
too much sodium.

51
00:02:57,261 --> 00:03:00,260
This is a pretty big piece, so we'll see what happens.

52
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:13,740
It's pretty impressive.

53
00:03:13,840 --> 00:03:16,180
The other element in salt, chlorine,

54
00:03:16,280 --> 00:03:20,120
may not be as explosive, but it can be equally dangerous.

55
00:03:20,220 --> 00:03:25,760
In its natural state, chlorine is a gas and poisonous.

56
00:03:25,761 --> 00:03:29,200
In World War I, both sides used it as a weapon,

57
00:03:29,300 --> 00:03:33,760
contributing to 91,000 gas-related deaths.

58
00:03:33,860 --> 00:03:36,340
Although chlorine and sodium can be deadly independent

59
00:03:36,440 --> 00:03:40,240
of one another, together they form the benign substance

60
00:03:40,340 --> 00:03:43,840
that is ultimately a driving force of life.

61
00:03:43,940 --> 00:03:45,940
We all need salt to survive.

62
00:03:46,040 --> 00:03:49,480
The adult human body contains over half a pound of it,

63
00:03:49,580 --> 00:03:52,980
enough to fill three large shakers.

64
00:03:52,981 --> 00:03:56,920
Our bodies are basically big electrical machines.

65
00:03:57,020 --> 00:03:58,760
And the salt is the electrolyte that

66
00:03:58,860 --> 00:04:02,060
keeps everything in balance, keeps the electric charges

67
00:04:02,160 --> 00:04:03,560
going in and out of the cells.

68
00:04:03,660 --> 00:04:07,060
It aids in the functioning of our nerves, the brain,

69
00:04:07,160 --> 00:04:10,960
and almost every other system in the body.

70
00:04:11,060 --> 00:04:13,840
Because salt is crucial to our very existence,

71
00:04:13,940 --> 00:04:16,600
nature hardwired us to crave it.

72
00:04:16,700 --> 00:04:18,500
Salt is one of the basic tastes.

73
00:04:18,501 --> 00:04:23,140
And so humans have a preference for salt.

74
00:04:23,240 --> 00:04:26,680
It is an innate desire to consume salt.

75
00:04:26,780 --> 00:04:29,380
It also makes foods taste better.

76
00:04:29,480 --> 00:04:31,140
It's not just a salty taste.

77
00:04:31,240 --> 00:04:33,120
It's that it masks bitter taste.

78
00:04:33,220 --> 00:04:38,280
It enhances sweet taste, which makes the food more palatable.

79
00:04:38,380 --> 00:04:40,160
Since we need salt so much, it's

80
00:04:40,260 --> 00:04:43,160
a good thing we can find it almost anywhere on Earth,

81
00:04:43,260 --> 00:04:46,620
including vast deposits in the United States.

82
00:04:46,621 --> 00:04:49,020
Hundreds of millions of years ago,

83
00:04:49,120 --> 00:04:51,020
the developing North American landmass

84
00:04:51,120 --> 00:04:54,020
was covered by prehistoric seas.

85
00:04:54,120 --> 00:04:57,660
As the seas evaporated, they left their salt behind,

86
00:04:57,760 --> 00:05:01,060
which was later buried beneath layers of sediment.

87
00:05:01,160 --> 00:05:04,400
One huge deposit stretches from Michigan through Ohio

88
00:05:04,500 --> 00:05:06,100
into Pennsylvania and New York.

89
00:05:09,080 --> 00:05:10,980
And it's what miners in Cleveland, Ohio,

90
00:05:11,080 --> 00:05:13,840
have been busily extracting at the Cargill Salt Mine

91
00:05:13,940 --> 00:05:16,620
since 1962.

92
00:05:16,621 --> 00:05:20,860
The deposit they tap, 1,800 feet directly below Lake Erie,

93
00:05:20,960 --> 00:05:22,920
is just a fraction of the mother load of salt

94
00:05:23,020 --> 00:05:26,260
spanning four states.

95
00:05:26,360 --> 00:05:28,760
They must drive 3 and 1 1 miles in darkness

96
00:05:28,860 --> 00:05:29,960
to the excavation site.

97
00:05:38,080 --> 00:05:41,680
The salt they mine here is part of the roughly 46 million tons

98
00:05:41,780 --> 00:05:45,180
of salt produced annually in the US,

99
00:05:45,181 --> 00:05:49,080
One fifth of the world's supply.

100
00:05:49,180 --> 00:05:52,920
Down here, workers prep the blast site, where it remains

101
00:05:53,020 --> 00:05:56,620
a constant 70 degrees Fahrenheit.

102
00:05:56,720 --> 00:06:00,860
They remove all the loose salt and drive six-foot bolts

103
00:06:00,960 --> 00:06:02,860
into the roof of the chamber to reinforce it.

104
00:06:06,560 --> 00:06:12,340
Then they drill holes into the wall and insert explosives.

105
00:06:12,341 --> 00:06:15,840
Putting them in place takes about one hour.

106
00:06:15,940 --> 00:06:18,480
Once that's complete and the area's secure,

107
00:06:18,580 --> 00:06:19,740
the blast is set off.

108
00:06:22,420 --> 00:06:25,820
The blast usually dislodges between 1,100 and 1,200

109
00:06:25,920 --> 00:06:30,020
tons of salt, which then travels by conveyor

110
00:06:30,120 --> 00:06:33,560
to a series of crushers and screeners.

111
00:06:33,660 --> 00:06:36,260
It reduces the chunks to less than half an inch in size.

112
00:06:39,560 --> 00:06:41,060
When it leaves the mule, it's essentially

113
00:06:41,061 --> 00:06:42,300
a finished product.

114
00:06:42,400 --> 00:06:47,100
It's taken to the shaft bottom where it's loaded into our

115
00:06:47,200 --> 00:06:51,940
production skips, placed it to the surface at a rate of 20

116
00:06:52,040 --> 00:06:52,940
tons per skip.

117
00:06:55,780 --> 00:06:57,640
With three shifts working daily,

118
00:06:57,740 --> 00:07:01,980
the mine produces an excess of 13,000 tons a day,

119
00:07:02,080 --> 00:07:05,420
and over 3 million tons each year.

120
00:07:05,520 --> 00:07:08,160
But the miners here aren't even close to exhausting their little

121
00:07:08,161 --> 00:07:12,660
corner of the underground salty stash.

122
00:07:12,760 --> 00:07:16,500
We have a 9,000-acre track under lease currently.

123
00:07:16,600 --> 00:07:19,300
And essentially, with the multiple levels,

124
00:07:19,400 --> 00:07:21,700
it seems that we can mine.

125
00:07:21,800 --> 00:07:24,820
We have 150 years of reserves remaining.

126
00:07:24,920 --> 00:07:27,520
Even some of history's earliest salt mines,

127
00:07:27,620 --> 00:07:30,720
open and accessible to this day, have a wealth of salt

128
00:07:30,820 --> 00:07:32,220
still available.

129
00:07:32,320 --> 00:07:34,860
One of the oldest in Wieliczka, Poland

130
00:07:34,960 --> 00:07:37,460
dates back to the 13th century.

131
00:07:37,461 --> 00:07:40,960
its caverns extend as deep as 1,100 feet.

132
00:07:41,060 --> 00:07:43,560
At the 300-foot level, the medieval miners

133
00:07:43,660 --> 00:07:48,500
left an astonishing signature from the vast deposit of salt.

134
00:07:48,600 --> 00:07:50,900
They sculpt at this chapel.

135
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:54,680
And everything here, from the chandeliers to the altar,

136
00:07:54,780 --> 00:07:56,080
is made of pure salt.

137
00:08:00,920 --> 00:08:04,480
In Silver Springs, New York, an hour south of Buffalo,

138
00:08:04,481 --> 00:08:07,880
Morton salt uses a different mining method.

139
00:08:07,980 --> 00:08:11,780
It doesn't require tons of explosives or an army of workers,

140
00:08:11,880 --> 00:08:13,480
but a flood of water.

141
00:08:13,580 --> 00:08:15,120
It's known as solution mining.

142
00:08:19,120 --> 00:08:21,300
A pipe extends more than 2,000 feet

143
00:08:21,400 --> 00:08:24,300
into a subterranean salt deposit.

144
00:08:24,400 --> 00:08:27,000
The pipe carries fresh water into the deposit,

145
00:08:27,100 --> 00:08:30,940
creating a pool of salt-intense brine.

146
00:08:31,040 --> 00:08:34,480
Then pumps force the brine up a second pipe to the surface.

147
00:08:34,481 --> 00:08:39,480
Next, the brine is treated chemically to remove impurities.

148
00:08:39,580 --> 00:08:43,220
The challenge now is to separate the salt from the water.

149
00:08:43,320 --> 00:08:45,620
They do that by boiling it.

150
00:08:45,720 --> 00:08:48,120
What you're looking at right here behind me

151
00:08:48,220 --> 00:08:51,840
is a set of four pans, four evaporated pans

152
00:08:51,940 --> 00:08:55,740
where we bring the raw brine in or the treated brine,

153
00:08:55,840 --> 00:08:58,580
and it boils through the pan.

154
00:08:58,680 --> 00:09:01,580
The vacuum pans operate like a pressure cooker,

155
00:09:01,581 --> 00:09:04,340
only in reverse, the lower atmospheric pressure in the

156
00:09:04,440 --> 00:09:08,320
vacuum allows water to boil at a lower temperature.

157
00:09:08,420 --> 00:09:12,720
Water normally boils at 212 degrees Fahrenheit.

158
00:09:12,820 --> 00:09:15,600
But these pans can do the trick as low as 100 degrees

159
00:09:15,700 --> 00:09:17,820
Fahrenheit.

160
00:09:17,920 --> 00:09:20,860
Steam from a boiler heats the first pan,

161
00:09:20,960 --> 00:09:24,500
boiling the brine at 225 degrees.

162
00:09:24,600 --> 00:09:28,000
That steam then travels to the second pan, where the brine

163
00:09:28,100 --> 00:09:31,580
boils at only 180 degrees.

164
00:09:31,581 --> 00:09:35,980
By the fourth and final pan, the brine boils at only 105 degrees.

165
00:09:38,620 --> 00:09:40,580
We're now in the lower pan room.

166
00:09:41,040 --> 00:09:45,060
This is where the salt water that comes off the bottom of the evaporation pan.

167
00:09:45,520 --> 00:09:46,900
This is called a slurry tank.

168
00:09:47,540 --> 00:09:51,380
And as you can see by looking at the brine coming out, it's a very milky color.

169
00:09:51,960 --> 00:09:54,620
And the reason it's milky is there's a lot of salt in that water.

170
00:09:54,621 --> 00:10:01,540
This is the slurry off the number four pan, which is the last of the four pans.

171
00:10:01,880 --> 00:10:07,200
The temperature has decreased so much by the time it got to this fourth pan that I can literally

172
00:10:07,201 --> 00:10:11,911
put my hand in here underneath the slurry and catch it.

173
00:10:11,912 --> 00:10:15,080
And you'll see as the water runs off,

174
00:10:15,081 --> 00:10:20,600
I have salt left in my hand. After the evaporation process,

175
00:10:20,601 --> 00:10:24,100
the salt still contains some water,

176
00:10:24,200 --> 00:10:27,100
so a filter wheel dries it off.

177
00:10:27,200 --> 00:10:30,340
The end result is common table salt.

178
00:10:30,440 --> 00:10:33,740
This process can also create the purest form of salt,

179
00:10:33,840 --> 00:10:37,460
99.9% sodium chloride.

180
00:10:37,560 --> 00:10:41,160
It's used as saline salt in emergency rooms

181
00:10:41,260 --> 00:10:45,100
and is part of kidney dialysis.

182
00:10:45,200 --> 00:10:47,460
Yet another method of producing salt

183
00:10:47,461 --> 00:10:51,760
taps the rich resources not beneath the ground,

184
00:10:51,860 --> 00:10:54,660
but in the Earth's oceans.

185
00:10:54,760 --> 00:10:58,000
In Newark, California, south of San Francisco,

186
00:10:58,100 --> 00:11:02,500
Cargill operates a solar evaporation plant.

187
00:11:02,600 --> 00:11:05,780
Three pumps send as much as 30,000 gallons per minute

188
00:11:05,880 --> 00:11:08,920
of seawater from San Francisco Bay

189
00:11:09,020 --> 00:11:12,160
to solar evaporation ponds, where the brine concentrates

190
00:11:12,260 --> 00:11:14,360
for four to five years.

191
00:11:14,460 --> 00:11:16,060
When ready, the concentrated brine

192
00:11:16,061 --> 00:11:20,460
travels to the facility's crystallizers, where it dries,

193
00:11:20,560 --> 00:11:22,800
forming crystals.

194
00:11:22,900 --> 00:11:26,000
This is a fully saturated solution of brine water,

195
00:11:26,100 --> 00:11:30,300
meaning that this water would not hold any more salt.

196
00:11:30,400 --> 00:11:35,420
It's approximately 25% sodium chloride, 75% water.

197
00:11:35,520 --> 00:11:39,920
The color of the brine is pink because of a microorganism that

198
00:11:40,020 --> 00:11:44,220
is in the brine that will only live at the salinity level.

199
00:11:44,221 --> 00:11:46,680
The crystallizer behind me is the final step

200
00:11:46,780 --> 00:11:48,860
in our process of making the salt.

201
00:11:48,960 --> 00:11:51,220
The salt water, once it's become saturated

202
00:11:51,320 --> 00:11:54,460
with sodium chloride, is pumped into the crystallizer,

203
00:11:54,560 --> 00:11:56,260
usually in the beginning of the summer months,

204
00:11:56,360 --> 00:11:57,960
what we call our making season.

205
00:11:58,060 --> 00:11:59,460
That's when the sunshine is the hottest,

206
00:11:59,560 --> 00:12:02,280
and there's usually the most amount of wind in the summertime.

207
00:12:02,380 --> 00:12:04,780
The wind stirs up the brines.

208
00:12:04,880 --> 00:12:07,480
As it blows, there's actually wave action.

209
00:12:07,580 --> 00:12:09,480
What we found is the more wind that blows,

210
00:12:09,580 --> 00:12:11,880
the bigger the crystals that will form.

211
00:12:11,980 --> 00:12:13,680
To gather the salt crystals,

212
00:12:13,681 --> 00:12:17,680
Cargill relies on a 20-ton piece of equipment called a harvester.

213
00:12:17,780 --> 00:12:20,680
There's actually a knife edge on the very bottom.

214
00:12:20,780 --> 00:12:26,120
It's seven feet wide, and it cuts about five inches of the salt.

215
00:12:26,220 --> 00:12:28,820
This is our largest crystallizer, where we harvest

216
00:12:28,920 --> 00:12:31,840
over 200,000 tons of salt.

217
00:12:31,940 --> 00:12:34,840
And we're going to be in this pond for about the next month

218
00:12:34,940 --> 00:12:36,840
harvesting salt.

219
00:12:36,940 --> 00:12:40,840
This salt is 99% purity even before it's been washed.

220
00:12:40,940 --> 00:12:43,040
It's actually safe to eat at this point.

221
00:12:43,041 --> 00:12:45,940
at this point.

222
00:12:46,040 --> 00:12:48,580
Tastes salty.

223
00:12:48,680 --> 00:12:52,280
Trucks take 30 tons of salt each to the wash plant,

224
00:12:52,380 --> 00:12:53,980
where it's dumped into a pit of brine water

225
00:12:54,080 --> 00:12:55,680
to remove any remaining impurities.

226
00:12:58,480 --> 00:13:00,820
The salt then goes through a screw washer

227
00:13:00,920 --> 00:13:03,300
to dewater the crystals.

228
00:13:03,400 --> 00:13:06,400
The dewatering screen drains the wash brine,

229
00:13:06,500 --> 00:13:10,040
and the salt is clean and ready to stack.

230
00:13:10,041 --> 00:13:13,140
The 600,000 tons produced here annually

231
00:13:13,141 --> 00:13:17,380
will be going into more than just salt shakers.

232
00:13:17,381 --> 00:13:20,620
Surprisingly, of all the salt produced in the US,

233
00:13:20,621 --> 00:13:24,060
only 5% is used for food.

234
00:13:24,061 --> 00:13:27,900
The number one use, consuming nearly half of America's supply,

235
00:13:27,901 --> 00:13:30,300
just might save your life in the dead of winter.

236
00:13:35,100 --> 00:13:38,140
Every year in the United States, snow and ice

237
00:13:38,141 --> 00:13:44,840
caused 600,000 traffic accidents and 2,200 fatalities.

238
00:13:44,940 --> 00:13:48,620
To counter the problem, workers apply about 16 million tons

239
00:13:48,720 --> 00:13:52,460
of salt on winter roads, taking advantage of salt's ability

240
00:13:52,560 --> 00:13:56,360
to melt through ice and snow.

241
00:13:56,460 --> 00:13:59,220
More than half of all the salt produced in America,

242
00:13:59,320 --> 00:14:01,660
nine times more than we sprinkle on our food,

243
00:14:01,760 --> 00:14:03,840
is used for this purpose.

244
00:14:03,940 --> 00:14:06,700
That includes most of the salt you see here at Cargill's salt

245
00:14:06,701 --> 00:14:09,600
facility in Cleveland.

246
00:14:09,700 --> 00:14:12,240
I'd say about 95% of the salt we produce

247
00:14:12,340 --> 00:14:16,540
is exclusively for the icing market.

248
00:14:16,640 --> 00:14:18,440
The salt travels straight from the mine

249
00:14:18,540 --> 00:14:21,640
to professional de-icers, like those here at North Country

250
00:14:21,740 --> 00:14:26,560
Snow and Ice Management in Glens Falls, New York.

251
00:14:26,660 --> 00:14:29,260
It's only the first snow of the season,

252
00:14:29,360 --> 00:14:30,660
but they're already hard at work.

253
00:14:33,660 --> 00:14:35,700
The salt doesn't attach to the ice,

254
00:14:35,701 --> 00:14:39,340
but instead dissolves within it.

255
00:14:39,440 --> 00:14:43,600
Salt molecules replace some water molecules in the ice,

256
00:14:43,700 --> 00:14:46,240
decreasing the number of water molecules able to bond

257
00:14:46,340 --> 00:14:47,840
into ice crystals.

258
00:14:47,940 --> 00:14:49,940
Once the ice becomes a salty solution,

259
00:14:50,040 --> 00:14:52,460
the newly created brine will penetrate the remaining ice

260
00:14:52,560 --> 00:14:55,920
down to the pavement, creating a brine sandwich with the road

261
00:14:56,020 --> 00:14:58,960
on bottom and the ice on top.

262
00:14:59,060 --> 00:15:02,100
The brine lowers the freezing point of water from 32 degrees

263
00:15:02,200 --> 00:15:05,700
Fahrenheit to about 2 degrees, throwing some of the ice.

264
00:15:05,701 --> 00:15:08,900
A plow then comes across the road, breaking up the ice

265
00:15:09,000 --> 00:15:11,440
so it can be removed.

266
00:15:11,540 --> 00:15:12,940
The most dangerous part of this job

267
00:15:13,040 --> 00:15:16,340
is not doing it right the first time.

268
00:15:16,440 --> 00:15:19,040
Any miscalculation can be the difference between life

269
00:15:19,140 --> 00:15:20,740
and death on the road.

270
00:15:20,840 --> 00:15:22,760
If I don't do my job, someone gets hurt,

271
00:15:22,860 --> 00:15:25,060
I probably don't have a job anymore.

272
00:15:25,160 --> 00:15:27,220
And we try to make sure everyone is safe when

273
00:15:27,320 --> 00:15:29,360
we're dealing with ice.

274
00:15:29,460 --> 00:15:32,000
But using more and more salt doesn't necessarily

275
00:15:32,100 --> 00:15:35,700
equal a successful de-icing mission.

276
00:15:35,701 --> 00:15:39,300
The proper application rate is about 300 pounds,

277
00:15:39,400 --> 00:15:41,500
250 pounds per acre.

278
00:15:41,600 --> 00:15:44,740
If you undersalt, the temperature at which salt works

279
00:15:44,840 --> 00:15:46,240
is going to increase.

280
00:15:46,340 --> 00:15:48,140
If you oversalt, the same thing.

281
00:15:48,240 --> 00:15:51,140
You are actually going to lose the effectiveness of the salt

282
00:15:51,240 --> 00:15:52,660
by putting too much down.

283
00:15:52,760 --> 00:15:56,860
So there is a sweet spot as far as application is concerned.

284
00:15:56,960 --> 00:15:59,800
Salt may be a cheap and efficient de-icing agent,

285
00:15:59,900 --> 00:16:01,460
but there's a downside.

286
00:16:01,560 --> 00:16:04,860
The salt water it leaves behind, especially on roads

287
00:16:04,861 --> 00:16:06,900
where salt is applied to liberally,

288
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:10,500
can cause massive corrosion problems for cars.

289
00:16:10,600 --> 00:16:12,200
Automotive corrosion costs Americans

290
00:16:12,300 --> 00:16:15,400
in excess of $23 billion each year,

291
00:16:15,500 --> 00:16:19,100
or $115 for every motorist.

292
00:16:19,200 --> 00:16:21,000
And the problem doesn't stop there.

293
00:16:21,100 --> 00:16:23,620
The salt water can also seep into the seams of bridges

294
00:16:23,720 --> 00:16:25,920
and other metallic road structures,

295
00:16:26,020 --> 00:16:28,820
accelerating their corrosion.

296
00:16:28,920 --> 00:16:31,260
Cleveland's Cargill facility is doing its part

297
00:16:31,360 --> 00:16:34,820
to solve the problem by adding an extra carbon

298
00:16:34,860 --> 00:16:36,860
to the de-icing salt it produces.

299
00:16:36,960 --> 00:16:38,800
It's a product we make called clearlane.

300
00:16:38,900 --> 00:16:40,260
There is a corrosion inhibitor in it,

301
00:16:40,360 --> 00:16:43,200
so on a bridge deck, if it gets into the cracks,

302
00:16:43,300 --> 00:16:45,900
it would not deteriorate the steel or the rebar in the bridge

303
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:47,900
deck as quickly.

304
00:16:48,000 --> 00:16:53,020
As prized as salt is for this purpose today,

305
00:16:53,120 --> 00:16:56,020
2,000 years ago, it was treasured as the most vital part

306
00:16:56,120 --> 00:16:58,120
of the human diet.

307
00:16:58,220 --> 00:17:02,160
Since salt is essential to life, it became a prime mover,

308
00:17:02,161 --> 00:17:05,860
inextricably tied to the march of history.

309
00:17:05,960 --> 00:17:09,600
The military security of the Roman Empire hinged on it.

310
00:17:09,700 --> 00:17:12,440
The Roman soldiers were paid in part in salt,

311
00:17:12,540 --> 00:17:15,440
and from which we get the name salary.

312
00:17:15,540 --> 00:17:17,780
Societies that controlled salt trade routes

313
00:17:17,880 --> 00:17:20,620
wielded vast economic power.

314
00:17:20,720 --> 00:17:22,940
In the 13th century, merchants in Venice

315
00:17:23,040 --> 00:17:26,220
gained control of an important maritime trade route passing

316
00:17:26,320 --> 00:17:27,620
through the Adriatic Sea.

317
00:17:27,720 --> 00:17:30,320
The revenue they amassed from regulating salt traffic

318
00:17:30,321 --> 00:17:33,120
funded Venice's hydraulic system that prevented the city

319
00:17:33,220 --> 00:17:35,160
from washing away.

320
00:17:35,260 --> 00:17:37,120
Many of the major roads in Europe and Asia

321
00:17:37,220 --> 00:17:39,760
trace the same paths as the old salt trade routes.

322
00:17:42,860 --> 00:17:45,340
And in the Sahara Desert, merchants

323
00:17:45,440 --> 00:17:47,800
still transport salt mined from beneath the sands

324
00:17:47,900 --> 00:17:50,400
along the same route traveled by their ancestors

325
00:17:50,500 --> 00:17:52,840
1,000 years ago.

326
00:17:52,940 --> 00:17:56,140
But the salt then came at a steeper price.

327
00:17:56,240 --> 00:17:59,580
It was traded at one point ounce for ounce with gold

328
00:17:59,581 --> 00:18:02,320
because it was so hard to get.

329
00:18:02,420 --> 00:18:04,220
At no time was salt more valued

330
00:18:04,320 --> 00:18:07,720
as a staple of human survival than in war.

331
00:18:07,820 --> 00:18:09,920
During America's Civil War, Union troops

332
00:18:10,020 --> 00:18:12,520
targeted salt storage and salt production facilities

333
00:18:12,620 --> 00:18:16,460
in the South, knowing that if they cut off the salt supply,

334
00:18:16,560 --> 00:18:18,640
the Confederacy would wither.

335
00:18:18,740 --> 00:18:21,040
But the North failed to disrupt enough of the Southern salt

336
00:18:21,140 --> 00:18:24,580
traffic to make a difference and had to win victory

337
00:18:24,680 --> 00:18:28,180
with more conventional strategies.

338
00:18:28,181 --> 00:18:31,520
Companies like Morton Salt know we need salt just as much now

339
00:18:31,620 --> 00:18:33,520
as we did then.

340
00:18:33,620 --> 00:18:35,820
Some of these silos at its manufacturing facility

341
00:18:35,920 --> 00:18:41,320
in Silver Springs, New York, hold as much as 600 tons.

342
00:18:41,420 --> 00:18:42,720
The salt produced here will not only

343
00:18:42,820 --> 00:18:44,840
find its way to homes and restaurants,

344
00:18:44,940 --> 00:18:49,740
but also into processed foods as a preservative and flavoring.

345
00:18:49,840 --> 00:18:53,380
A whopping 77% of the sodium Americans consume

346
00:18:53,480 --> 00:18:57,180
comes from eating processed foods, about 15 times more

347
00:18:57,181 --> 00:19:01,660
than the amount we ingest by sprinkling it on our meals.

348
00:19:01,760 --> 00:19:04,520
This helps explain why each American consumes about 15

349
00:19:04,620 --> 00:19:08,660
pounds of sodium each year, 50% more than the USDA's maximum

350
00:19:08,760 --> 00:19:10,900
recommendation.

351
00:19:11,000 --> 00:19:12,460
This excess of salt can contribute

352
00:19:12,560 --> 00:19:14,060
to strokes and hypertension.

353
00:19:14,160 --> 00:19:17,500
But on the plus side for salt, since 1924,

354
00:19:17,600 --> 00:19:19,060
Morton and some other salt makers

355
00:19:19,160 --> 00:19:22,280
have been adding a key element to help keep you healthy,

356
00:19:22,380 --> 00:19:23,840
iodine.

357
00:19:23,940 --> 00:19:25,900
We all need iodine, mostly because it

358
00:19:25,901 --> 00:19:28,900
because it helps regulate the thyroid gland,

359
00:19:29,000 --> 00:19:31,700
which controls how quickly the body burns energy.

360
00:19:31,800 --> 00:19:34,700
Iodizing salt was the great public health achievement,

361
00:19:34,800 --> 00:19:38,140
along with sanitizing water in the 20th century.

362
00:19:38,240 --> 00:19:40,940
Although we'd known for a century before that

363
00:19:41,040 --> 00:19:44,160
that iodine was important to the function of the thyroid gland,

364
00:19:44,260 --> 00:19:48,460
we didn't understand that salt could so readily satisfy that

365
00:19:48,560 --> 00:19:49,260
need.

366
00:19:49,261 --> 00:19:51,260
People eat a predictable amount of salt,

367
00:19:51,360 --> 00:19:54,200
so you can meter the amount of iodine that they get

368
00:19:54,201 --> 00:19:56,800
by putting it in the salt, knowing how much salt they'll

369
00:19:56,900 --> 00:19:57,740
have.

370
00:19:57,840 --> 00:19:59,700
We've all added a little salt to a meal

371
00:19:59,800 --> 00:20:02,400
for just the right flavor.

372
00:20:02,500 --> 00:20:04,740
But prior to refrigeration, adding salt

373
00:20:04,840 --> 00:20:08,180
was the best way to keep food from spoiling.

374
00:20:08,280 --> 00:20:11,280
Salt preserves food by absorbing the moisture bacteria need

375
00:20:11,380 --> 00:20:14,420
to multiply.

376
00:20:14,520 --> 00:20:16,560
Some historians contend that the United States

377
00:20:16,660 --> 00:20:19,160
owes its very existence to salt's value as a food

378
00:20:19,260 --> 00:20:22,420
preservative.

379
00:20:22,421 --> 00:20:25,260
Before the Revolution, the American colonies prospered

380
00:20:25,360 --> 00:20:28,120
and grew in part because salt made possible the lucrative

381
00:20:28,220 --> 00:20:31,560
North Atlantic cod industry.

382
00:20:31,660 --> 00:20:34,060
Salt preserves cod unusually well,

383
00:20:34,160 --> 00:20:37,200
because its flesh is almost entirely devoid of fat.

384
00:20:37,300 --> 00:20:41,200
Fat resists salt, slowing the rate of preservation.

385
00:20:41,300 --> 00:20:42,980
Using salt, colonial fishermen were

386
00:20:43,080 --> 00:20:47,080
able to preserve and export vast amounts of cod,

387
00:20:47,180 --> 00:20:49,880
helping to power the economy of a young America,

388
00:20:49,980 --> 00:20:52,420
surging toward independence.

389
00:20:52,421 --> 00:20:55,760
And the proven preserver still comes in handy today.

390
00:20:55,860 --> 00:20:58,060
Salt is used in foods today as a preservative.

391
00:20:58,160 --> 00:21:01,860
Hams and foods like that that are cured in salt

392
00:21:01,960 --> 00:21:05,260
are done for the same reason of not only imparting flavor,

393
00:21:05,360 --> 00:21:07,760
but to have a preserving effect.

394
00:21:07,860 --> 00:21:10,880
Still, our closest encounter with salt on a daily basis

395
00:21:10,980 --> 00:21:12,280
is when we reach for the shaker.

396
00:21:14,880 --> 00:21:16,820
And there's one restaurant where salting your food

397
00:21:16,920 --> 00:21:19,120
is no insult to the chef.

398
00:21:19,220 --> 00:21:21,780
He encourages it.

399
00:21:21,781 --> 00:21:25,620
Located in the Ritz-Carlton Hotel on Amelia Island, Florida,

400
00:21:25,720 --> 00:21:30,680
salt incorporates salt into every aspect of the meal,

401
00:21:30,780 --> 00:21:34,900
from food prep to cooking to flavoring at the table.

402
00:21:35,000 --> 00:21:37,220
But when you say, pass the salt here,

403
00:21:37,320 --> 00:21:39,940
you'd better be more specific.

404
00:21:40,040 --> 00:21:42,800
At any given time, we have about 40 different salts

405
00:21:42,900 --> 00:21:44,440
in the restaurant.

406
00:21:44,540 --> 00:21:46,600
They come from all around the world.

407
00:21:46,700 --> 00:21:49,340
Some of the salts are made different by nature,

408
00:21:49,440 --> 00:21:51,080
which has added minerals to the deposits

409
00:21:51,081 --> 00:21:53,240
from which they're mined.

410
00:21:53,340 --> 00:21:55,320
Others owe their character to processes

411
00:21:55,420 --> 00:21:59,720
in which common salt is mixed with ingredients like clay

412
00:21:59,820 --> 00:22:02,920
or undergoes treatments like wood smoking.

413
00:22:03,020 --> 00:22:06,020
Activated charcoal is turned this salt black.

414
00:22:06,120 --> 00:22:07,800
And the large pyramid-shaped crystals

415
00:22:07,900 --> 00:22:10,140
make for a fun crunch.

416
00:22:10,240 --> 00:22:12,340
This salt is cypress flake salt.

417
00:22:12,440 --> 00:22:15,140
This is harvested from the Black Sea in the Mediterranean.

418
00:22:15,240 --> 00:22:17,440
We use this a lot of times either in salads

419
00:22:17,540 --> 00:22:20,080
or raw preparations because the quick pop of salt,

420
00:22:20,081 --> 00:22:22,040
but also the texture.

421
00:22:22,140 --> 00:22:24,380
Some salts here, like this one that contains

422
00:22:24,480 --> 00:22:28,120
a bamboo flavor, go beyond spicing up your meal.

423
00:22:28,220 --> 00:22:31,220
This is the Japanese aguni salt.

424
00:22:31,320 --> 00:22:33,620
It comes from a barrier island off the coast of Japan,

425
00:22:33,720 --> 00:22:34,820
Aguni Island.

426
00:22:34,920 --> 00:22:37,140
What we use this for in the restaurant

427
00:22:37,240 --> 00:22:39,200
is mainly with our coffee.

428
00:22:39,300 --> 00:22:42,740
When it's added to coffee, it actually lowers the acidity

429
00:22:42,840 --> 00:22:45,680
and mellows out the bitterness of coffee.

430
00:22:45,780 --> 00:22:49,040
One of the salts here, created by the restaurant's chefs,

431
00:22:49,041 --> 00:22:51,680
even adds a little flavor to the desserts.

432
00:22:51,780 --> 00:22:54,120
What we have here is just a little bit of dried lavender

433
00:22:54,220 --> 00:22:56,740
that we're going to put into our coffee grinder,

434
00:22:56,840 --> 00:23:00,080
a little bit of Bolivian Rose because it's very delicate salt,

435
00:23:00,180 --> 00:23:03,460
and lavender is a very delicate, fragrant flower.

436
00:23:03,560 --> 00:23:05,660
Basically, you just have a coffee grinder,

437
00:23:05,760 --> 00:23:07,700
your salt and your lavender in there,

438
00:23:07,800 --> 00:23:10,060
just keeping blending it until the lavender

439
00:23:10,160 --> 00:23:13,740
has been fully incorporated into the salt.

440
00:23:13,840 --> 00:23:16,540
What we're left with is a fragrant Bolivian Rose

441
00:23:16,541 --> 00:23:21,780
that we could use on sorbet, on ice cream, on dessert.

442
00:23:21,880 --> 00:23:24,780
As you're smacking your lips at the sight of all the salt here,

443
00:23:24,880 --> 00:23:27,780
are you wondering what kind is the priciest?

444
00:23:27,880 --> 00:23:30,280
The most expensive one that I have come across

445
00:23:30,380 --> 00:23:32,280
is a Danish Viking salt.

446
00:23:32,380 --> 00:23:35,000
It's about $150 a pound.

447
00:23:35,100 --> 00:23:36,900
We don't necessarily use it in the restaurant

448
00:23:37,000 --> 00:23:39,100
because of the cost of it.

449
00:23:39,200 --> 00:23:42,200
The restaurant's salt philosophy runs so deep,

450
00:23:42,300 --> 00:23:45,340
it's literally in the very air you breathe.

451
00:23:45,341 --> 00:23:50,040
Out in the front of our room, we feature a Himalayan salt piece.

452
00:23:50,460 --> 00:23:54,180
As the salt heats up, it actually ionizes the air,

453
00:23:54,480 --> 00:23:57,100
and it's supposed to make you feel happier, a little bit stress-free.

454
00:23:57,420 --> 00:24:01,400
But also, it's kind of the centerpiece of what we're trying to represent,

455
00:24:01,800 --> 00:24:03,080
you know, a journey through salt.

456
00:24:03,920 --> 00:24:05,980
As much as we crave and value salt,

457
00:24:07,180 --> 00:24:10,540
it also makes our most abundant source of water undrinkable.

458
00:24:10,541 --> 00:24:13,580
But if you're willing to pay a $10 million electric bill,

459
00:24:13,820 --> 00:24:15,280
technology offers a solution.

460
00:24:15,340 --> 00:24:22,340
The Earth's surface is a staggering 326 million cubic

461
00:24:22,440 --> 00:24:26,880
miles of water, and all of it is filled with salt.

462
00:24:26,980 --> 00:24:29,280
If you gathered up all that salt,

463
00:24:29,380 --> 00:24:34,560
it would cover the continents to a depth of 500 feet.

464
00:24:34,660 --> 00:24:36,500
Conceptually, that's a fitting image,

465
00:24:36,600 --> 00:24:38,860
because where do you think the salt in the oceans came from

466
00:24:38,960 --> 00:24:40,800
in the first place?

467
00:24:40,801 --> 00:24:44,100
the oceans came from in the first place.

468
00:24:44,200 --> 00:24:46,040
Ultimately, the salt that is in the ocean

469
00:24:46,140 --> 00:24:47,340
came from the land.

470
00:24:47,440 --> 00:24:52,740
Salt journeyed to the oceans thanks to rain.

471
00:24:52,840 --> 00:24:55,440
When rain falls, the surrounding air

472
00:24:55,540 --> 00:24:57,280
causes it to be slightly acidic.

473
00:24:57,380 --> 00:24:59,780
As it strikes land, the acid in the rain

474
00:24:59,880 --> 00:25:02,560
then erodes and breaks down rocks.

475
00:25:02,660 --> 00:25:07,120
The runoff carries dissolved salts to streams and rivers,

476
00:25:07,220 --> 00:25:10,800
which lead to the ocean.

477
00:25:10,801 --> 00:25:14,140
Only fresh water in the ocean evaporates,

478
00:25:14,240 --> 00:25:17,400
returning to the atmosphere.

479
00:25:17,500 --> 00:25:22,740
Since the salt is too heavy to evaporate, it stays put.

480
00:25:22,840 --> 00:25:27,680
The ocean's salt content is 3.5%.

481
00:25:27,780 --> 00:25:30,320
But that's nothing compared to 20,000 years ago,

482
00:25:30,420 --> 00:25:32,860
when scientists estimate it was nearly double that.

483
00:25:32,960 --> 00:25:35,500
That's because a sizable portion of the Earth's fresh water

484
00:25:35,600 --> 00:25:38,320
was locked up in glaciers during the last ice age,

485
00:25:38,321 --> 00:25:41,220
and that left less water to dilute the salt entering

486
00:25:41,320 --> 00:25:43,060
the ocean.

487
00:25:43,160 --> 00:25:45,920
Today's ocean salt content is more than twice that of the fish

488
00:25:46,020 --> 00:25:51,560
that live there, which have a salinity of 1.5%.

489
00:25:51,660 --> 00:25:55,040
But the fish can drink the water and survive

490
00:25:55,140 --> 00:25:57,440
because their gills are able to process the water,

491
00:25:57,540 --> 00:25:59,780
removing some of the salt.

492
00:25:59,880 --> 00:26:02,280
As the evolutionary descendants of fish,

493
00:26:02,380 --> 00:26:06,220
we humans have a slightly lower salinity, 0.9%.

494
00:26:06,221 --> 00:26:09,220
So although we need and can easily ingest salt,

495
00:26:09,320 --> 00:26:11,960
drinking salt water is very harmful to us.

496
00:26:12,060 --> 00:26:14,720
You can't drink salt water because drinking salt water

497
00:26:14,820 --> 00:26:17,960
would actually dehydrate you, cause kidney failure,

498
00:26:18,060 --> 00:26:19,860
and you would die.

499
00:26:19,960 --> 00:26:22,260
You would have to drink about one gallon of salt water

500
00:26:22,360 --> 00:26:25,480
for your body to cross the fatal threshold.

501
00:26:25,580 --> 00:26:28,880
The only way to make ocean water safe for human consumption,

502
00:26:28,980 --> 00:26:34,620
short of developing gills, is a process known as desalination.

503
00:26:34,621 --> 00:26:37,660
Tampa, Florida is home to the largest seawater desalination

504
00:26:37,760 --> 00:26:40,120
plant in North America.

505
00:26:40,220 --> 00:26:43,360
It provides water for 2.5 million people.

506
00:26:43,460 --> 00:26:47,160
The plant here is co-located with a power plant.

507
00:26:47,260 --> 00:26:49,060
In their process of making electricity,

508
00:26:49,160 --> 00:26:52,280
they take 1.4 billion gallons from Tampa Bay

509
00:26:52,380 --> 00:26:54,080
and use it to cool their boilers.

510
00:26:54,180 --> 00:26:57,740
We take 44 million gallons and make 25 million gallons

511
00:26:57,840 --> 00:26:59,080
of drinking water.

512
00:26:59,180 --> 00:27:00,920
Before the facility takes on the task

513
00:27:01,020 --> 00:27:03,320
of removing the salt from the seawater,

514
00:27:03,321 --> 00:27:05,560
The water must travel through a series of filters

515
00:27:05,660 --> 00:27:10,760
to remove other impurities, leaving only the salt in the water.

516
00:27:10,860 --> 00:27:13,600
Now comes the most crucial step of all.

517
00:27:13,700 --> 00:27:17,160
That water is now ready to go into the reverse osmosis

518
00:27:17,260 --> 00:27:19,840
building, which is right behind me.

519
00:27:19,940 --> 00:27:22,680
It is forced through these long tubes.

520
00:27:22,780 --> 00:27:26,180
Each one of those tubes has membranes in it.

521
00:27:26,280 --> 00:27:29,420
Here, this is a cutaway of the membranes.

522
00:27:29,421 --> 00:27:33,720
membranes. This is actually about half of the length of a full

523
00:27:33,721 --> 00:27:37,720
membrane. The water is put under high pressure, about 1,000 psi,

524
00:27:37,721 --> 00:27:42,060
and it's pushed through this end of the membrane.

525
00:27:42,061 --> 00:27:43,860
The intense pressure forces the water

526
00:27:43,861 --> 00:27:47,280
across the spiral membrane, which consists of multiple layers

527
00:27:47,281 --> 00:27:50,780
dotted with molecular-sized pores.

528
00:27:50,781 --> 00:27:54,880
The salt particles are too large to pass through the pores,

529
00:27:54,881 --> 00:27:58,120
but the pure water molecules are small enough to fit through

530
00:27:58,121 --> 00:28:01,020
into a collector tube at the end of the membrane.

531
00:28:01,120 --> 00:28:06,020
The salty concentrate exits through openings on the side.

532
00:28:06,120 --> 00:28:09,240
And pumps move 19 million gallons of the concentrate

533
00:28:09,340 --> 00:28:12,600
per day back to the electric power plant

534
00:28:12,700 --> 00:28:17,400
to be diluted with salt water before it's returned to the bay.

535
00:28:17,500 --> 00:28:19,500
After the water has undergone a final round

536
00:28:19,600 --> 00:28:22,280
of chemical treatment, the plant has met its daily goal

537
00:28:22,380 --> 00:28:26,240
of producing 25 million gallons of drinkable water.

538
00:28:26,241 --> 00:28:29,080
Well, here we are at the end of the desalination facility.

539
00:28:29,180 --> 00:28:31,980
It's hard to believe that only one hour ago that water

540
00:28:32,080 --> 00:28:33,980
entered the plant from the bay.

541
00:28:34,080 --> 00:28:40,080
And now I can tap it and drink it.

542
00:28:40,180 --> 00:28:42,480
Cheers.

543
00:28:42,580 --> 00:28:45,000
But desalinated water has a downside.

544
00:28:45,100 --> 00:28:47,200
One of the things about doing seawater desal

545
00:28:47,300 --> 00:28:48,400
is the cost.

546
00:28:48,500 --> 00:28:51,340
It's the operating costs here that are very expensive.

547
00:28:51,440 --> 00:28:54,400
We have a $17 million budget to run this plant.

548
00:28:54,401 --> 00:28:58,580
almost 10 million of that is just to pay our electric bill.

549
00:28:58,680 --> 00:29:00,340
To ease energy costs, the plant

550
00:29:00,440 --> 00:29:04,780
utilizes a reusable energy technique.

551
00:29:04,880 --> 00:29:07,640
After the salty concentrate exits the reverse osmosis

552
00:29:07,740 --> 00:29:13,020
membrane, it maintains a pressure of over 900 psi.

553
00:29:13,120 --> 00:29:15,880
The high pressure is used to spin a turbine,

554
00:29:15,980 --> 00:29:17,560
creating electricity.

555
00:29:17,660 --> 00:29:20,120
We are getting an upwards of 35% energy

556
00:29:20,220 --> 00:29:22,420
recovery from that system.

557
00:29:22,421 --> 00:29:25,060
Still, desalinated water remains the most expensive

558
00:29:25,160 --> 00:29:26,860
to produce.

559
00:29:26,960 --> 00:29:31,600
It costs the company $3.38 per 1,000 gallons,

560
00:29:31,700 --> 00:29:34,860
compared to its groundwater production at about $1

561
00:29:34,960 --> 00:29:39,540
and its surface water at about $2.

562
00:29:39,640 --> 00:29:42,400
Although it may only account for 10% of Tampa's drinking

563
00:29:42,500 --> 00:29:44,980
water, desalinated water currently

564
00:29:45,080 --> 00:29:49,920
stands apart as one of the very few drought-proof sources.

565
00:29:49,921 --> 00:29:54,360
I do think back to the famous John F. Kennedy quotation

566
00:29:54,460 --> 00:29:58,860
about if we could find a way to economically desalinate

567
00:29:58,960 --> 00:30:01,660
water, that it would be one of the greatest achievements

568
00:30:01,760 --> 00:30:03,160
of mankind.

569
00:30:03,260 --> 00:30:05,460
And we've done it on a scale that we haven't seen

570
00:30:05,560 --> 00:30:08,080
in the United States.

571
00:30:08,180 --> 00:30:11,780
As of 2008, the Tampa plant is the only large-scale

572
00:30:11,880 --> 00:30:14,080
desalination facility in the US.

573
00:30:14,180 --> 00:30:18,380
What other coastal states are exploring the possibilities?

574
00:30:18,381 --> 00:30:21,220
California alone has plans for as many as 20 plants.

575
00:30:24,060 --> 00:30:25,720
There are times when adding salt to water

576
00:30:25,820 --> 00:30:28,820
can be just as beneficial as removing it.

577
00:30:28,920 --> 00:30:31,260
Case in point, swimming pools.

578
00:30:31,360 --> 00:30:33,400
The conventional method of disinfecting the water

579
00:30:33,500 --> 00:30:35,500
is to add chlorine.

580
00:30:35,600 --> 00:30:38,800
But too much chlorine in a pool will irritate your eyes

581
00:30:38,900 --> 00:30:41,800
and can even make you sick.

582
00:30:41,900 --> 00:30:44,440
In Moorpark, California, Jandy Pool Products

583
00:30:44,540 --> 00:30:47,040
makes a revolutionary device that makes it virtually

584
00:30:47,041 --> 00:30:49,940
impossible to over-chlorinate your pool.

585
00:30:50,040 --> 00:30:51,940
A salt chlorinator.

586
00:30:52,040 --> 00:30:53,540
The key to its operation?

587
00:30:53,640 --> 00:30:57,980
Breaking down common table salt to generate its own chlorine.

588
00:30:58,080 --> 00:31:02,280
Simply add 300 pounds of ordinary table salt to the pool

589
00:31:02,380 --> 00:31:04,400
and let it dissolve.

590
00:31:04,500 --> 00:31:06,300
It flows into the chlorinator positioned

591
00:31:06,400 --> 00:31:08,600
between the pool's filter and outlet.

592
00:31:08,700 --> 00:31:11,300
Electrodes powered by low DC voltage

593
00:31:11,400 --> 00:31:13,500
emit an electric current that splits the salt

594
00:31:13,600 --> 00:31:16,340
into sodium and chlorine.

595
00:31:16,341 --> 00:31:19,180
Over time, the small amounts of chlorine return to the pool

596
00:31:19,181 --> 00:31:24,000
and kill any bacteria, algae, or viruses.

597
00:31:24,920 --> 00:31:28,180
We do add about 300 pounds of salt to the average pool,

598
00:31:28,320 --> 00:31:30,840
but it's not enough salt to reach your taste threshold.

599
00:31:31,220 --> 00:31:33,180
So it tastes like ordinary water.

600
00:31:34,240 --> 00:31:35,500
To give you a comparison,

601
00:31:35,880 --> 00:31:38,320
it's only one-third the saltiness of your tears.

602
00:31:38,680 --> 00:31:41,540
And because the salinity is closer to your own tears,

603
00:31:42,020 --> 00:31:42,920
there's no eye irritation.

604
00:31:43,520 --> 00:31:45,020
And unlike old-fashioned pools,

605
00:31:45,021 --> 00:31:47,520
There's no chlorine smell.

606
00:31:47,620 --> 00:31:49,120
But how does the system prevent you

607
00:31:49,220 --> 00:31:50,960
from over-chlorinating?

608
00:31:51,060 --> 00:31:52,960
Once the chlorine has done its job,

609
00:31:53,060 --> 00:31:55,560
it doesn't hang around in the pool.

610
00:31:55,660 --> 00:31:58,560
A saltwater pool is a model of recycling efficiency.

611
00:31:58,660 --> 00:32:01,840
It reuses the salt molecules over and over.

612
00:32:01,940 --> 00:32:04,200
The chlorine does the sanitizing,

613
00:32:04,300 --> 00:32:06,180
and when it's done doing the sanitizing,

614
00:32:06,280 --> 00:32:09,980
recombines with the sodium to become salt again.

615
00:32:10,080 --> 00:32:11,620
But salt can do a lot more for you

616
00:32:11,720 --> 00:32:14,320
than make pool fun go more swimmingly.

617
00:32:14,321 --> 00:32:17,360
Would you believe this chunk of salt can be the key to electrifying

618
00:32:17,460 --> 00:32:18,160
your house?

619
00:32:22,160 --> 00:32:27,560
Just like metals, salts can melt if you get them hot enough.

620
00:32:27,660 --> 00:32:30,400
That makes them perfect for an industrial application known

621
00:32:30,500 --> 00:32:32,240
as the melanite process.

622
00:32:32,340 --> 00:32:36,200
It's been around since the early 20th century.

623
00:32:36,300 --> 00:32:38,440
Burlington Engineering in Orange, California

624
00:32:38,540 --> 00:32:41,620
uses the melanite process to add a layer of protection

625
00:32:41,720 --> 00:32:43,180
to steel parts.

626
00:32:43,181 --> 00:32:47,520
that increases hardness and slows corrosion and wear.

627
00:32:47,620 --> 00:32:50,020
Melaniding takes two varieties of salt,

628
00:32:50,120 --> 00:32:52,180
but neither is sodium chloride.

629
00:32:52,280 --> 00:32:55,720
However, they are cousins of salt.

630
00:32:55,820 --> 00:32:58,620
They both contain sodium as the metal component,

631
00:32:58,720 --> 00:33:03,440
but it's bound to nitrogen in one salt and carbon in the other.

632
00:33:03,540 --> 00:33:06,040
During the process, nitrogen and carbon

633
00:33:06,140 --> 00:33:08,740
penetrate the surface of the steel parts,

634
00:33:08,840 --> 00:33:11,680
fusing with the steel.

635
00:33:11,681 --> 00:33:23,098
We see the melanite process used in a huge variety of industries, anywhere from automotive and using it on things like crankshafts to give longer wear life on bearing

636
00:33:23,099 --> 00:33:24,680
surfaces and camshafts.

637
00:33:25,420 --> 00:33:32,840
We do aftermarket automotive work where we do all different types and sizes of flywheels that will wear longer and last longer.

638
00:33:32,841 --> 00:33:36,840
We see it in the oil drilling industry.

639
00:33:36,940 --> 00:33:41,840
We have parts that go out on oil rigs out in the middle of the ocean and out in the middle of the desert.

640
00:33:41,940 --> 00:33:50,840
And so they have to hold up in a very severe environment where not only wear resistance but corrosion resistance is very important.

641
00:33:50,940 --> 00:33:58,840
The first step in the process is we take the parts and we put them into racks or fixtures and then we take them and we wash them.

642
00:33:58,940 --> 00:34:01,840
The parts travel to a preheat oven.

643
00:34:01,841 --> 00:34:05,080
preheat oven where they'll be heated to 800 degrees Fahrenheit.

644
00:34:05,081 --> 00:34:08,680
Without this step, the parts could deform in the intense

645
00:34:08,681 --> 00:34:11,880
heat of the salt bath.

646
00:34:11,881 --> 00:34:14,680
So now the parts have finished going through the preheat

647
00:34:14,681 --> 00:34:15,381
process.

648
00:34:15,382 --> 00:34:17,080
And now that they're at 800 degrees,

649
00:34:17,081 --> 00:34:20,800
we're going to bring them around and immerse them into the salt

650
00:34:20,801 --> 00:34:23,300
bath nitriding bath.

651
00:34:23,301 --> 00:34:25,300
The parts will remain in this initial bath

652
00:34:25,301 --> 00:34:27,300
for 90 minutes.

653
00:34:27,301 --> 00:34:31,300
So after we've gone through the first step of the SPN bath,

654
00:34:31,301 --> 00:34:33,100
We now come into the second step.

655
00:34:33,200 --> 00:34:36,700
We're diffusing nitrogen and carbon primarily

656
00:34:36,800 --> 00:34:37,800
into the steel.

657
00:34:37,900 --> 00:34:39,700
And this is where the carbon goes in.

658
00:34:39,800 --> 00:34:43,840
And it'll be in this bath for approximately 30 minutes.

659
00:34:43,940 --> 00:34:46,940
A water quench completes the process.

660
00:34:47,040 --> 00:34:49,860
But some steel parts need an even smoother surface

661
00:34:49,960 --> 00:34:53,860
or more corrosion resistance and require a polish.

662
00:34:53,960 --> 00:34:56,660
For that, they get all shook up.

663
00:34:56,760 --> 00:34:59,300
We'll take parts, and we'll put them in a vibratory tub,

664
00:34:59,301 --> 00:35:02,140
which polishes the parts and moves them around.

665
00:35:02,240 --> 00:35:04,300
Depending on the parts that we're polishing,

666
00:35:04,400 --> 00:35:06,940
sometimes we'll use a media like this,

667
00:35:07,040 --> 00:35:11,480
which is a corncob media that's impregnated with resin.

668
00:35:11,580 --> 00:35:13,740
The melanite process improves some items

669
00:35:13,840 --> 00:35:15,820
in surprising ways.

670
00:35:15,920 --> 00:35:18,220
The molten salt baths can smooth out the interior

671
00:35:18,320 --> 00:35:21,920
of a gun barrel, resulting in less friction for the bullets.

672
00:35:22,020 --> 00:35:24,460
If it has a lower drag coefficient,

673
00:35:24,560 --> 00:35:25,860
it gets less hot.

674
00:35:25,960 --> 00:35:29,300
And a gun barrel that is less hot is more accurate.

675
00:35:29,301 --> 00:35:34,300
Of their products, the folks at Burlington Engineering

676
00:35:34,400 --> 00:35:37,300
like to say the same thing automaker Henry Ford once said

677
00:35:37,400 --> 00:35:39,300
about his Model T.

678
00:35:39,400 --> 00:35:42,840
It's any color you want as long as it's black.

679
00:35:42,940 --> 00:35:45,340
One of molten salt's properties,

680
00:35:45,440 --> 00:35:50,360
its ability to retain intense heat over long periods of time,

681
00:35:50,460 --> 00:35:54,860
might just revolutionize the world of renewable energy.

682
00:35:54,960 --> 00:35:57,360
An innovative system using molten salt

683
00:35:57,361 --> 00:36:01,520
molten salt as an energy storage device called Solar Trace is now

684
00:36:01,521 --> 00:36:02,840
under construction in Spain.

685
00:36:04,680 --> 00:36:08,680
It's based on the success of an earlier prototype, Solar II,

686
00:36:08,681 --> 00:36:12,380
which operated in California's Mojave Desert between 1995

687
00:36:12,381 --> 00:36:14,200
and 1999.

688
00:36:15,480 --> 00:36:19,820
While sodium chloride becomes molten at 1,474 degrees

689
00:36:19,821 --> 00:36:22,440
Fahrenheit and could easily do the job,

690
00:36:23,680 --> 00:36:26,860
engineers incorporated other salts into Solar II's operations

691
00:36:26,861 --> 00:36:31,600
because they melt at a much lower temperature.

692
00:36:31,700 --> 00:36:35,340
A 300-foot hollow tower was encircled by more than 1,900

693
00:36:35,440 --> 00:36:37,980
mirrors called heliostats.

694
00:36:38,080 --> 00:36:41,200
The heliostats tracked the sun, reflecting its energy

695
00:36:41,300 --> 00:36:43,580
to a receiver mounted on the tower.

696
00:36:43,680 --> 00:36:46,980
The receiver transmitted intense heat inside the tower

697
00:36:47,080 --> 00:36:50,920
to salt composed of sodium nitrate and potassium nitrate.

698
00:36:51,020 --> 00:36:54,560
At over 1,000 degrees Fahrenheit, the salt became molten

699
00:36:54,561 --> 00:36:57,600
molten and glowed to a storage tank.

700
00:36:57,700 --> 00:36:59,960
To produce energy, pumps conveyed the salt

701
00:37:00,060 --> 00:37:03,040
through a steam generator consisting of a water tank

702
00:37:03,140 --> 00:37:05,440
and piping similar to a radiator.

703
00:37:05,540 --> 00:37:10,300
The steam then spun turbines, generating electrical energy.

704
00:37:10,400 --> 00:37:13,740
The system could produce up to 10 megawatts,

705
00:37:13,840 --> 00:37:19,260
enough to power 10,000 homes, using mineral salts no different

706
00:37:19,360 --> 00:37:23,020
than common garden fertilizer.

707
00:37:23,021 --> 00:37:26,060
The salts allowed for energy to remain stored when clouds covered

708
00:37:26,160 --> 00:37:29,860
the sun or at night.

709
00:37:29,960 --> 00:37:35,600
Once operational, solar trace will produce 17 megawatts.

710
00:37:35,700 --> 00:37:37,760
As the world continues to go green,

711
00:37:37,860 --> 00:37:41,980
salt promises to spice things up.

712
00:37:42,080 --> 00:37:43,580
Molten salts can hold energy.

713
00:37:46,680 --> 00:37:49,420
But if you're looking to use some energy of your own

714
00:37:49,421 --> 00:37:54,060
At record-breaking speeds, nothing beats an open salt flat.

715
00:38:00,160 --> 00:38:05,560
You may think the ocean is salty at 3.5% salinity,

716
00:38:05,660 --> 00:38:09,980
but that's nothing compared to the Great Salt Lake in Utah.

717
00:38:10,080 --> 00:38:13,180
The southern half of the lake is 15%,

718
00:38:13,280 --> 00:38:18,680
and the north is 25%, seven times saltier than the ocean.

719
00:38:18,681 --> 00:38:21,480
The Great Salt Lake is the largest inland body of salt water

720
00:38:21,481 --> 00:38:22,920
in the Western Hemisphere.

721
00:38:22,921 --> 00:38:25,720
A railroad causeway bisects the lake,

722
00:38:25,721 --> 00:38:29,060
preventing fresh water from entering the north side.

723
00:38:29,061 --> 00:38:30,560
Through openings in the causeway,

724
00:38:30,561 --> 00:38:33,500
only concentrated salt water from the southern half

725
00:38:33,501 --> 00:38:36,540
can enter the northern half of the lake.

726
00:38:36,541 --> 00:38:38,840
The difference in salinity gives each half of the lake

727
00:38:38,841 --> 00:38:41,840
unique properties, including different colors

728
00:38:41,841 --> 00:38:44,400
and even different life forms.

729
00:38:44,401 --> 00:38:46,100
In the south half of the lake, you

730
00:38:46,101 --> 00:38:48,740
You get a blue-green color to it.

731
00:38:48,741 --> 00:38:53,300
That's usually due to the blue-green algae.

732
00:38:53,301 --> 00:38:55,780
When you get over the north half of the lake,

733
00:38:55,781 --> 00:38:58,640
it will be an absolutely pink or a red color,

734
00:38:58,641 --> 00:39:03,520
and that is due to the halophilic bacteria.

735
00:39:03,521 --> 00:39:06,920
The lake's high salinity makes life for fish impossible.

736
00:39:06,921 --> 00:39:09,160
But for more than a century, that hasn't prevented

737
00:39:09,161 --> 00:39:12,420
the tourist industry from taking advantage of the area.

738
00:39:12,421 --> 00:39:16,100
For years and years, this was a favorite place for resorts.

739
00:39:16,101 --> 00:39:19,540
The most famous of the lake's resorts, Salt Air, opened in

740
00:39:19,640 --> 00:39:21,500
1893.

741
00:39:21,600 --> 00:39:25,740
Now a museum, it was once billed as a Coney Island of the West.

742
00:39:25,840 --> 00:39:30,140
It peaked in the 1920s and closed in the 50s.

743
00:39:30,240 --> 00:39:32,480
While the resorts had their amusements, the real fun was

744
00:39:32,580 --> 00:39:34,960
in the lake itself.

745
00:39:35,060 --> 00:39:37,680
Swimmers discovered that the rich salt content made them

746
00:39:37,780 --> 00:39:42,000
so buoyant it was impossible to sink.

747
00:39:42,100 --> 00:39:45,360
The Great Salt Lake is all that remains of Lake Bonneville, a

748
00:39:45,361 --> 00:39:49,700
a vast freshwater lake that existed some 30,000 years ago.

749
00:39:49,800 --> 00:39:55,000
It covered 20,000 square miles, stretching from Nevada to Idaho.

750
00:39:55,100 --> 00:39:58,600
And 100 miles from Salt Lake, where its waters once rippled,

751
00:39:58,700 --> 00:40:02,420
is now the sterile emptiness of the Bonneville Salt Flats.

752
00:40:02,520 --> 00:40:05,520
When it went away over the course of thousands of years,

753
00:40:05,620 --> 00:40:08,620
what was left behind were the salts that had been dissolved

754
00:40:08,720 --> 00:40:09,720
in the water.

755
00:40:09,820 --> 00:40:12,760
It's become kind of a focal point for a lot of folks

756
00:40:12,761 --> 00:40:14,560
as they think about the United States

757
00:40:14,660 --> 00:40:15,960
from throughout the world.

758
00:40:16,060 --> 00:40:19,400
They want to see the salt flats.

759
00:40:19,500 --> 00:40:23,140
Though a tourist attraction, the desolate 160 square miles

760
00:40:23,240 --> 00:40:26,180
of the flats pose a danger without adequate water,

761
00:40:26,280 --> 00:40:28,540
food, and equipment.

762
00:40:28,640 --> 00:40:31,820
Anyone getting stuck out here would never survive.

763
00:40:31,920 --> 00:40:33,880
The land is so smooth and flat, you

764
00:40:33,980 --> 00:40:35,280
can see the curvature of the Earth.

765
00:40:38,380 --> 00:40:41,360
But instead of danger, some see the vast open flats

766
00:40:41,361 --> 00:40:46,760
think one thing. Speed.

767
00:40:46,761 --> 00:40:52,420
You might think the salt creates great traction, but the opposite is true. Salt

768
00:40:52,421 --> 00:40:57,540
decreases traction by 12 to 15%, making driving on it a bit similar to driving on

769
00:40:57,541 --> 00:41:02,960
snow or ice. But unlike normal roads and highways, you won't find any potholes

770
00:41:02,961 --> 00:41:11,240
here. If any holes do occur, the solution is simple. Just pack in more salt. People

771
00:41:11,241 --> 00:41:13,480
from all over for the World of Speed Week,

772
00:41:13,580 --> 00:41:14,980
held every September.

773
00:41:15,080 --> 00:41:17,740
It's a time when everyday drivers and motorcyclists

774
00:41:17,840 --> 00:41:21,780
get to open up their engines to see just how fast they can go.

775
00:41:21,880 --> 00:41:27,180
Well, the first year we came out in 2001, we went like 196.

776
00:41:27,280 --> 00:41:31,600
And then it took four more years to get the last four miles

777
00:41:31,700 --> 00:41:32,500
an hour.

778
00:41:32,600 --> 00:41:35,940
And once you go 200 out here, then you

779
00:41:36,040 --> 00:41:37,600
want to go a little faster.

780
00:41:37,700 --> 00:41:39,940
We started out here about 2003.

781
00:41:39,941 --> 00:41:42,180
We've been about here every year since then,

782
00:41:42,280 --> 00:41:44,380
so two or three times a year.

783
00:41:44,480 --> 00:41:46,680
We've had the car up to 220.

784
00:41:46,780 --> 00:41:48,720
The Salt Flats have long been a destination

785
00:41:48,820 --> 00:41:51,480
for speed record setters.

786
00:41:51,580 --> 00:41:54,060
John Cobb set the first of many land speed records

787
00:41:54,160 --> 00:41:59,920
at the Flats in 1935, a blistering 368 miles per hour.

788
00:42:04,440 --> 00:42:07,060
Since then, many drivers have made the pilgrimage

789
00:42:07,160 --> 00:42:09,040
to the Salt Flats with the intent

790
00:42:09,041 --> 00:42:10,040
setting new records.

791
00:42:16,280 --> 00:42:18,580
The drivers we caught up with weren't all expecting

792
00:42:18,680 --> 00:42:23,180
to set records or for their cars to win any beauty contests.

793
00:42:23,280 --> 00:42:24,680
Here's the way I feel about it.

794
00:42:24,780 --> 00:42:27,800
I only care about how fast it goes, not how pretty it looks.

795
00:42:27,900 --> 00:42:30,200
The course has a little ruts in it.

796
00:42:30,300 --> 00:42:32,800
The car likes to drift a little on the course.

797
00:42:32,900 --> 00:42:35,840
And it really makes you feel better if it will go where you

798
00:42:35,940 --> 00:42:36,740
point it.

799
00:42:39,040 --> 00:42:42,280
Still, they're quite happy to take advantage of yet another

800
00:42:42,380 --> 00:42:44,080
surprising dimension of salt.

801
00:42:46,540 --> 00:42:48,680
The salt is an incredibly abundant resource,

802
00:42:48,780 --> 00:42:49,880
but it's not endless.

803
00:42:49,980 --> 00:42:52,680
And we have to manage this in such a manner

804
00:42:52,780 --> 00:42:55,420
that future generations can come out here

805
00:42:55,520 --> 00:42:59,160
and marvel at the same thing that you're marveling at today.

806
00:42:59,260 --> 00:43:01,100
The salt abundance of these flats

807
00:43:01,200 --> 00:43:05,400
belies its scarcity throughout most of human history.

808
00:43:05,500 --> 00:43:08,200
So the next time you're asked, don't just

809
00:43:08,201 --> 00:43:09,860
Don't just pass the salt.

810
00:43:09,960 --> 00:43:13,200
Pause and be thankful your salary isn't just the contents

811
00:43:13,300 --> 00:43:15,700
of the shaker in your hand.

